< Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or to their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. We find that small reasoning models mostly get the arithmetic right and the bid wrong. Six open models of up to 14B parameters choose among candidate bids with win curves from eBay prices, under fixed token budgets, and are scored on held-out auctions against an exact calculator. They recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead. Most of the rest is lost after the trace has already written the best option's value as the largest. The Qwen models run out of tokens before finishing the comparison: allowed to stop on their own within 4{,}096 tokens, they and DeepSeek-8B recover 99--100\% at under 1.2\textcent{} per decision, so for them the token cap is the main loss. Granite-8B finishes the comparison and then answers against it, following list position; Phi-mini's trace usually names the right option and its letter answer departs from it. Wrong answers lean toward the bid most likely to win, which costs bidders under pay-your-bid pricing, also with rivals in first-price equilibrium, but not under eBay's own rule. Sending answers that disagree with the trace's own numbers to a calculator recovers 84--100\% of the gap under nested cross-validation.
---
> Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or to their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. Six open models of up to 14B parameters choose among candidate bids with win curves from eBay prices, under fixed token budgets, and are scored on held-out auctions against an exact calculator. At 768 tokens they recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead (64\% for Phi-mini when the answer its trace states is used). Arithmetic errors are a small share of the rest for five models. The Qwen models and DeepSeek-8B lose mainly to the token cap: allowed to stop on their own, they recover 99--100\% for about a cent per decision. Phi-mini's trace states the right option, but its letter answer departs from it. Granite-8B's loss is split between arithmetic and passing over the best option it wrote, and that split is uncertain. Wrong answers lean toward the bid most likely to win, which costs bidders under pay-your-bid pricing, also against rivals in first-price equilibrium. Sending answers that disagree with the trace's own numbers to a calculator recovers 84--100\% of the gap under nested cross-validation.
