< \paragraph{Scenarios.}
< We use 628 eBay auctions of Palm Pilot PDAs, Xbox consoles and Cartier watches collected by Jank and Shmueli \cite{jank2010modeling}, in the Kaggle copy \cite{onlineauctionsdataset}, split 70/30 within product. Per product, 18 private values $v$ are spaced evenly from the 35th percentile of construction-sample closing prices to 1.25 times the 95th. For each value and menu size $K\in\{5,7\}$, two candidate sets of \$5-step bids are drawn below $v$ within the 2nd--98th price percentiles, as a random contiguous window or a random sample, never conditioned on the optimum: 216 scenarios. Each bid shows $\hat p(b)$, the share of construction prices at or below $b$; the chosen bid is scored with the same estimator on the 189 held-out auctions (41--103 per product). eBay prices come from proxy bidding, so these are realistic empirical win curves rather than first-price win rates; models and calculator see the same landscape.
< 
< \paragraph{Measures.}
< \emph{Utility ratio} $q=U(\hat b)/\max_b U(b)$ with expected profit $U(b)=p(b)(v-b)$ under held-out probabilities $p$. The calculator returns $\arg\max_b \hat p(b)(v-b)$ and scores $q=0.981$; random choice scores 0.711. \emph{Gap recovered} at budget $c$ is $(q(c)-q(0))/(q_{\text{calc}}-q(0))$; the \emph{dollar gap} uses $U$. Intervals resample the 18 value families within each product (2{,}000 draws); also resampling the three products widens them (Phi-mini: 10--59\%) but leaves every model below the calculator, as does resampling the auctions behind the displayed and scoring curves ($\ge$96\% of 1{,}000 draws). For a second scoring curve (\emph{FP eq.}) we treat each closing price as the second-highest of its $n$ bidders' values, fit a lognormal value distribution $G$ per product by maximum likelihood, and let rivals bid the first-price equilibrium $\beta(x)=x-\int_0^x G^{N-1}/G(x)^{N-1}$ with $N=n+1$, so $b$ wins with probability $G(\beta^{-1}(b))^{n}$ averaged over auctions.
---
