> \paragraph{Measures}
> \emph{Utility ratio} $q=U(\hat b)/\max_b U(b)$ with expected profit $U(b)=p(b)(v-b)$ under held-out probabilities $p$. The calculator returns $\arg\max_b \hat p(b)(v-b)$ and scores $q=0.981$; random choice scores 0.711. \emph{Gap recovered} at budget $c$ is $(q(c)-q(0))/(q_{\text{calc}}-q(0))$. Intervals resample the 18 value families within each product (2{,}000 draws). Resampling the three products as well widens them, and so does resampling the auctions behind both curves, but every model stays below the calculator. For a second scoring curve (\emph{FP eq.}) we treat each closing price as the second-highest of its $n$ bidders' values, fit a lognormal value distribution $G$ per product by maximum likelihood, and let rivals bid the first-price equilibrium $\beta(x)=x-\int_0^x G(t)^{N-1}\,dt\,/\,G(x)^{N-1}$ with $N=n+1$, so $b$ wins with probability $G(\beta^{-1}(b))^{n}$ averaged over auctions.
