< \paragraph{Models and protocol.}
< The checkpoints are Phi-4-mini-reasoning (Phi-mini), Qwen3-4B, -8B and -14B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-\allowbreak Llama-8B (DeepSeek-8B) and Granite-3.2-8B-\allowbreak Instruct (Granite-8B) with reasoning on \cite{microsoft2025phireasoning,yang2025qwen3,guo2025deepseek,ibmgranite2025}. For each budget $c$ in $\{0,\allowbreak 64,\allowbreak 128,\allowbreak 256,\allowbreak 512,\allowbreak 768\}$ we force exactly $c$ reasoning tokens by suppressing end-of-sequence, then append an answer prompt and take the most likely option letter; the free-text answer is also recorded. The prompt lists the options in randomized order, gives the objective as ``(private value $-$ b) $\times$ estimated win probability'' and asks the model not to infer the answer from option position. Greedy decoding runs under three option orders per scenario (648 decisions per model and budget); five sampled seeds run at 128, 384 and 768 tokens (seed s.d. of $q$ at most 0.017); a run without forcing lets each model stop within 1{,}024 tokens, and a second lets Qwen3-4B, -8B, -14B and DeepSeek-8B stop within 4{,}096. It ran on another GPU with the same software and weights; there, ten forced-768 decisions per model gave the original choice in 38 of 40. A 768-token decision takes 9--35 seconds and its tokens cost $c_{768}=0.19$--$0.75$\textcent{} at RunPod rates \cite{runpod2026pricing}; break-even is $\text{B/e}=c_{768}/(q(768)-q(0))$, the best expected profit above which the expected gain $\max_b U(b)\,(q(768)-q(0))$ covers $c_{768}$. A synthetic version \cite{krishna2010auction} uses the same protocol on 216 scenarios with values 8--99 and integer bids, against two rivals with uniform values on 0--99 who bid two thirds of them, so payoffs are exact.
---
> \paragraph{Models and protocol}
> The checkpoints are Phi-4-mini-reasoning (Phi-mini), Qwen3-4B, -8B and -14B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-\allowbreak Llama-8B (DeepSeek-8B) and Granite-3.2-8B-\allowbreak Instruct (Granite-8B) with reasoning on \cite{microsoft2025phireasoning,yang2025qwen3,guo2025deepseek,ibmgranite2025}. For each budget $c$ in $\{0,\allowbreak 64,\allowbreak 128,\allowbreak 256,\allowbreak 512,\allowbreak 768\}$ we force exactly $c$ reasoning tokens by suppressing end-of-sequence, then append an answer prompt and take the most likely option letter. The prompt lists the options in randomized order, gives the objective as ``(private value $-$ b) $\times$ estimated win probability'' and asks the model not to infer the answer from option position. Greedy decoding runs under three option orders per scenario (648 decisions per model and budget). Runs without forcing let each model stop within 1{,}024 tokens and, for the Qwen models and DeepSeek-8B, within 4{,}096; seed and GPU checks are in the README. The 768 tokens of one decision cost $c_{768}=0.19$--$0.75$\textcent{} at RunPod rates \cite{runpod2026pricing}; the \emph{break-even} $c_{768}/(q(768)-q(0))$ is the best expected profit above which they pay for themselves. A synthetic version uses the same protocol in the standard uniform first-price setting \cite{krishna2010auction}: 216 scenarios with values 8--99 and integer bids, against two rivals with uniform values on 0--99 who bid two thirds of them, so payoffs are exact.
