< \paragraph{Trace parsing.}
< The \emph{text reader} does no arithmetic: it splits the trace at each option's mention and takes the last result number written in that option's segment, skipping the inputs. A second parser marks an option \emph{evaluated} when the trace states its true expected profit within 2\%. Both are code, so every trace is classified without annotators. An option's written value is its true value when the parser finds it and the reader's number otherwise, so a value counts as miscomputed only if the true one appears nowhere; this bounds arithmetic errors from below. Counting every value the reader reads differently from the truth as miscomputed bounds them from above. Where both methods find a value, the reader's equals the true one in 86--95\% of options.
---
> \paragraph{Trace parsing and gap categories}
> Two pieces of code read each trace; no annotator is involved. The \emph{text reader} splits the trace at each option's mention and takes the last result number in that segment. A \emph{matcher} marks an option evaluated when the trace states its true expected profit within 2\%. An option's written value is its true value when the matcher finds it and the reader's number otherwise; this bounds arithmetic errors from below, and counting every misread value as miscomputed bounds them from above. Where both find a value, they agree in 86--95\% of options. Each wrong 768-token answer falls in the first category that applies:
> \begin{itemize}\setlength\itemsep{0pt}
> \item \emph{Not reached}: the trace never writes the best option's value (cut off before it).
> \item \emph{Arithmetic}: the trace's own values rank another option above the best (a miscomputed profit).
> \item \emph{Cut off}: the best option is written and ranked first, the list is unfinished, and the answer is another bid (the worked example).
> \item \emph{Passed over}: the same, but the list is finished.
> \end{itemize}
> The \emph{oracle reader} picks, among options whose true value appears in the trace, the one with the highest true value; it uses the answer key and is only an upper bound.
