< \paragraph{Forced reasoning recovers part of the calculator's value.}
< Forcing 768 tokens recovers 42--85\% of the calculator's lead (Table~\ref{tab:main}), almost all of it above 256 tokens; without reasoning the models are close to random. In the 1{,}024-token run, where most traces except Granite-8B's hit the cap, recovery rises to 72--96\% for five models, mostly by finishing the list, but stays at 49\% for Phi-mini. The Dollar column ranks models differently because Cartier dominates the dollars and the 0.711 random baseline compresses $q$.
< 
< \paragraph{Letting the model finish.}
< With a 4{,}096-token cap, almost every trace of Qwen3-4B, -8B, -14B and DeepSeek-8B stops on its own (at most 1.1\% hit the cap), after a median of 980--1{,}390 tokens (Figure~\ref{fig:main}a). The Qwen models then pick the calculator's option in 99.7--100\% of decisions and recover 100\% [100, 100] of the gap; DeepSeek-8B recovers 99\% [98, 100], and 21 of its 25 wrong answers pass over a finished list. The tokens cost 0.45--1.11\textcent{} per decision, 1.5--2.1 times $c_{768}$, so break-even rises only to 2.0--4.8\textcent{}, while the smallest best expected profit in any scenario (\$1.75) is 39--130 times B/e at 768 tokens and the median (\$48) over 1{,}000 times. For these models the fix is not to cap reasoning; the readers below matter where a cap is imposed for cost or latency.
