> \paragraph{Budget: the Qwen models and DeepSeek-8B}
> These models lose mainly to the token cap. Their gap is mostly \emph{not reached} or \emph{cut off} (Table~\ref{tab:traces}), and greedy forced traces at 512 tokens are prefixes of those at 768, so we can watch a list finish: when it does, they answer correctly in 84--89\% of cases. Allowed to stop on their own within 4{,}096 tokens, they rarely hit the cap (at most 1.1\%), stop after a median of 980--1{,}390 tokens, and recover 99--100\% of the gap (Figure~\ref{fig:main}a). Those tokens cost 0.45--1.11\textcent{} per decision, while the smallest best expected profit in any scenario (\$1.75) is 39--130 times the break-even at 768 tokens. For these models the fix is not to cap reasoning.
