< \paragraph{Which wrong bid?}
< Wrong answers are not random. DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models favour the highest bid on the menu, the one most likely to win: it accounts for 40--48\% of their wrong answers against about 18\% by chance, and these choices carry most of their loss. Phi-mini favours the first-listed option and Granite-8B the runner-up. One logistic regression over all models explains passing over, on the 3{,}519 traces at 768 tokens that write the best option's value and rank it first (errors clustered by scenario). The pull toward the highest bid is not a difficulty effect: with the best-to-runner-up profit gap, list position, $K$, value, product and model held fixed, passing over is a third as likely when the best option is itself the highest bid (odds ratio 0.36 [0.28, 0.46]). A larger profit gap (0.36 per unit of relative gap) and a finished list (0.38) make it rarer, $K=7$ doubles it (1.89), and value has no effect; a scenario random effect gives the same estimates. Moving the best option from first to last raises the odds 18-fold for Phi-mini and 4.4-fold for Qwen3-8B and cuts them to 0.15 for Granite-8B; the Phi-mini and Granite-8B patterns replicate on the synthetic auctions.
---
> \paragraph{Readout: Phi-mini}
> Phi-mini's trace states the right option, but its letter answer departs from it. A rule-based parser reads the last answer the trace itself states (``the highest expected profit is Option C''). It agrees with the letter in 96--98\% of Qwen traces but in only 57\% of Phi-mini's. There, the stated choice is the best option in 94\% of decisions and the letter in 55\%. Scoring the stated answer raises Phi-mini's recovery from 42\% to 64\% (Table~\ref{tab:main}); 95\% of its remaining loss comes from the 40\% of traces that state no answer.
