< \paragraph{What the wrong bids cost.}
< Under pay-your-bid pricing the pull toward high bids raises sellers' expected revenue by \$18k--\$83k per 1{,}000 decisions and lowers bidders' surplus by \$15k--\$36k, mostly on Cartier. Under eBay's rule, where the winner pays about the closing price, the same choices raise both. Under the FP eq.\ curve, where rivals also bid in equilibrium, moving the models' answers to the calculator's lowers expected seller revenue by \$13k--\$55k and raises bidder surplus by \$12k--\$28k per 1{,}000 decisions, and wrong answers overbid in 47--66\% of cases.
---
> \paragraph{Which wrong bids, and their cost}
> Wrong answers lean toward the highest bid on the menu, the one most likely to win. For DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models it accounts for 40--48\% of wrong answers against about 18\% by chance. A logistic regression over all models (3{,}519 traces that write the best value and rank it first; errors clustered by scenario) shows this is not a difficulty effect: holding the profit gap, position, $K$, value, product and model fixed, passing over is a third as likely when the best option is itself the highest bid (odds ratio 0.36 [0.28, 0.46]). A finished list makes passing over rarer (0.38) and $K=7$ doubles it (1.89); the README gives the full table. Under pay-your-bid pricing these choices lower bidders' surplus by \$15k--\$36k per 1{,}000 decisions, mostly on Cartier. Against rivals in first-price equilibrium the calculator's bids would raise it by \$12k--\$28k. Under eBay's rule, where the winner pays about the closing price, higher bids do not hurt the bidder.
