< \paragraph{Reading the trace.}
< A text reader that takes the largest value the trace wrote recovers more of the gap than the model's own answer (Table~\ref{tab:main}), but those figures are in-sample, and on the synthetic traces it hurts four models, so it is no replacement for the answer. It works as an audit: sending decisions where reader and answer disagree to the calculator recovers 84--100\% of the gap when the reader's settings are chosen with one product held out (nested cross-validation), 84--100\% when they are chosen on synthetic traces only, and 81--100\% on synthetic traces with settings chosen on eBay, at 18--57\% of decisions. Ranked by how far the answer's written value falls below the largest, it beats triggers by low letter probability, unfinished lists and random calls at every call rate (Figure~\ref{fig:main}c): sending 20\% of decisions recovers 89\% of the gap on average, against 81\%, 78\% and 74\%. Why not always call the calculator, or let the model finish? A bidder who builds its own agent should; the audit is for an overseer who sees a third-party agent's reasoning and bid but cannot change its prompt or tools. Since the overbids it corrects are seller revenue (\$12k--\$78k per 1{,}000 decisions under pay-your-bid), its natural user is the bidder's operator, or a platform whose fee depends on advertiser value rather than short-run revenue.
---
> \paragraph{Audit}
> Checking the answer against the trace's own numbers catches most errors. The text reader alone is no replacement for the answer: its gains in Table~\ref{tab:main} are in-sample, and it hurts four models on synthetic traces. As an audit it works: sending decisions where reader and answer disagree to the calculator recovers 84--100\% of the gap when the reader's settings are chosen with one product held out, and also when they are chosen on synthetic traces only. At every call rate it beats triggers by low letter probability, unfinished lists or chance (Figure~\ref{fig:main}c); sending 20\% of decisions recovers 89\% of the gap against at most 81\%. A bidder who builds its own agent could simply call the calculator or let the model finish; the audit is for an overseer who sees a third-party agent's reasoning and bid but cannot change its tools. Since the overbids it corrects are seller revenue, its natural user is the bidder's operator, or a platform whose fee depends on advertiser value.
